[[ch10-handbook-day-one]]
== Handbook: Day 1, Starting an MGO

This chapter assumes accepting a quest detailed in the chapter, "Handbook: The Fellowship of The Ring".

To recap, your quest is to find thirty people, a fellowship, and then to practice trusting those people creating a ring of trust: a Fellowship Of the Ring.

But how?

First thing to understand is that an MGO has two states: managed and unmanaged.

A managed MGO needs to meet all the requirements of a management project. This chapter and your first quest is about how to establish an unmanaged MGO. Relevant here is establishing a company of exactly thirty people.

Second thing to understand is that as an engineering project then building out an unmanaged MGO will have benefits every step of the way. The benefits don't kick in once the quest is completed. The benefits are continuously gained.

The first step you could take is to realize the benefits of pooling resources.

In the United States a corporation is a legal entity with much of the same rights as a person. Corporations take advantage of this legal entity to pool resources. Corporations can open bank accounts and buy products and services the same as any individual. Further, corporations give discounts based on bulk or large size volumes. Costco is an example of this where a Costco customer initially had to have a corporate account to get past the front door.

So your first step of your quest is to form a legal corporation. The goal is to save money by pooling resources. The corporation can act as an extended Costco. The corporation pools money from its members and everyone saves money. Saving money is a real world incentive to entice people to join your MGO company. Just be advised that fees and taxes apply. For example California requires every corporation to pay an annual $800 fee. Cities also require fees and taxes. Be sure to investigate and be aware of any fees and taxes the corporation is obligated to pay each year regardless of the amount of money earned or loss. Given this then you might want to wait to file the paperwork. Instead start a campaign to find the thirty people before starting the corporation. Once the thirty people are ready then the annual taxes and fees can be spread out amongst the thirty people. Of course if you or anyone in your company is willing to pay the taxes and fees individually then you can start the company then.

Corporations generally have two types of members: directors and employees. The simplest form of corporation is to have everyone be a director, no employees. Federal and State laws mandate certain director roles: President, Treasurer and so on. Note that these roles are paper roles only for legal purposes. They have no bearing on the operation of the company. As a New Democracy company then theobjective is one person, one vote. Each person is a director and each director has one vote. However, since this is still unmanaged you can still opt to form your corporation as a traditional hierarchy. Although given this is a New Democracy MGO then that kinda defeats one of the main purposes of New Democracy, which is to eliminate hierarchy. You can start a corporation and just ignore all aspects of New Democracy. This is the beauty of engineering versus ideology. Ideology requires adhering to the ideological worldview. Engineering is about function.

While saving money is an important benefit gained from starting a corporation it is not the most important. The most important is to find common cause, find your tribe. Every company needs to establish legal by-laws. These by-laws define how the corporation is going to conduct business.

For our purposes here,let's think about MGOs as if they were guilds; an association of people with similar interests or pursuits; an association of merchants or craftsmen.

What is your passion, trade or craft? Computers? Art? Electrician? Plumber? The notion is to start a guild that focuses on craftsmanship or skill. What comes next will be the formation of the company. Perhaps the company will just focus on training the skill and nothing else. Perhaps the company will become for-profit and sell goods and services. The interest and pursuit of the guild determines the structure of the company and its by-laws.

Following the establishment of your guild's particular type comes the charter. I would recommend keeping the charter separate from the by-laws. Derive the by-laws from the charter. A charter is simply a document that states explicitly the business of the guild and its purpose and obligations. This charter should be flexible when bringing on board new members. Everyone should have their distinct purpose and obligations reflected in the charter. The overall objective is New Democracy, not hierarchy. The charter becomes the honey to attract members. First is the guild type, second is the charter, and third is the charter being flexible and incorporating aspects of all new members.

You are not obligated to think about the company as a guild. I'm just suggesting this to get the ball rolling. A guild brings clarity and focus to the company overall and binds people to a common sphere of trust.

Now let's talk about dependents.
New Democracy is everyone in. Every single person on Earth should be able to join. This includes the old, the infant, the criminal and the homeless. Irreni requires a 50% dependency load as part of the managed requirements, or fifteen people. You are under no obligation to do so on day one being unmanaged. However, being family friendly will attract a wider audience. Corporations that are made up of entire families send a signal that your company is New Democracy. 
Now let's talk about contracts.
New Democracy is 100% voluntary where people can join and leave any time. Irreni requires managed MGO members to be under contract where the longest contract is five years. I recommend doing this on day one.
Here's an example contract:

I, ______, agree to join this company _______ for a duration of five years. There are only good-will benefits and bad-will penalties. This contract can be terminated before five years if the company approves of the termination per the by-laws. If I terminate the contract unilaterally and leave then the only penalty is bad-will for breach of trust. This breach of trust may be communicated to any person whatsoever for any reason to anyone who asks for a reference about fulfilling five years per the by-laws. Good-will references may be expected for fulfilling the five-year duration per the by-laws.

Of course there is nothing preventing a company from adding any incentives and such to the contract. What was just provided was the simplest contract about trust. Our quest is to build a fellowship ring, a ring of trust. The five year contract is one aspect of this trust.

Again, as an unmanaged MGO there is no obligation to use contracts for members. These contracts will only be required upon joining Irreni management.

Let's talk about the meta guild.

What is a meta guild? A meta guild is a guild that teaches other guilds how to form a guild. The business is guild making. I already mentioned that forming a corporation incurs an annual set of fees and taxes just for existing independent of financial gains and losses. That's about the least of it. In the United States we have for-profit and nonprofit corporations. We have LLCs, S-corps, C-corps, and seemingly endless types of corporations. Every corporation is going to need financial accounting with accounting software implemented. A meta guild is a guild that helps new guilds by supplying trained meta members. A meta guild reaches size thirty and becomes proficient at setting up guilds. They then splinter perhaps in half or one-third where the splinter joins a new guild. This new guild now has members well versed in the legal and technical aspects of running a corporation. Perhaps the meta guild members only sign a one-year contract to kick things off and then off they go to help another guild.

Meta guilds are going to be the fastest way to establish a mesh. A formal mesh is a collection of MGOs that coordinate via managed projects. An informal mesh is a collection of MGOs that coordinate however they see fit, but it is still recommended project contracts be used.

At first blush a meta guild may not seem a day one concern. However, teaching others how to start MGOs will quickly become one of the most common guild types. The objective is to have all eight billion people join an MGO. This will take generations to realize and meta guilds will be required all along the way.
